 ## 4. A permission must survive two changes of case
 
-**Change the source of unmet need while fixing its severity and the helper's burdens.** Suppose refusal by other capable helpers produces one shortfall, while unavoidable incapacity produces another. An self-direction-based permission would need to explain why this causal difference matters, if it does. A responsibility-allocation argument may treat it as central, but then must explain why the difference changes what the innocent patient may demand. These are separate questions. Combining two grounds does not excuse leaving either connection undefended.
